monorails are a sleek cool and  futuristic mode of transportation  but we don't seem to have many of them  running around in fact when compared to  conventional rail  monorails are almost non-existent and so  why is that well monorails actually tie  into this  contemporary agitation that all our  problems will be solved by  tech bros and their magic high-tech  solutions behind [ __ ] projects such  as the hyperloop or the boring company  loop  monorails are next in line and so why is  that  why are monorails such a bad idea  despite there being relatively  successful systems operating today  alright so problem number one of  basically any monorail system  is cross-compatibility meaning that if  you build a monorail line  only monorail trains will be able to run  on it which in practice limits your  system to a very specific passenger  train set  and so this here is a photo sphere image  of the otsuka station in tokyo  you can see the passenger platform and  the two tracks adjacent to it but next  to it there's another pair of tracks  now during my stay in tokyo i used to  take the ammonite line from this station  in the morning i would often see freight  trains going past the station on that  adjacent pair of tracks  now if this was a purely monorail system  this of course would not be possible but  then you could say oh well that's easy  let's just build a monorail and then  leave next to it two pairs of  conventional rare tracks so that both  freight trains and monorail trains can  run  but that idea doesn't really work either  the third and the fourth tracks are used  to divert trains too  should there be a problem with the first  or the second you know if the train  breaks down or something or if a switch  goes out  then trains can go around the station  you know of course if you had two  monorail tracks and two conventional  rail tracks you're kind of stuck with  those two you cannot divert any of the  trains to any other tracks  whereas with a fully conventional rail  system you could just switch them over  speaking of which problem number two  switches  now this might seem like a banal thing  at first but monorail switches are one  of the most  awkward and contrived things you can  build in transportation  if that thing gets stuck during  switching or there's any other  malfunction  the entire monorail line would need to  be shut down for hours possibly  so that the repair team can actually get  out there and somehow reach the  malfunction switch  which would most likely include  industrial alpinist stunts  now in contrast if a conventional rail  switch goes out it's much easier to  reach and the problem is usually  resolved within one hour  and if it's an older construction you  can either send the mic out to hammer it  back into place  so that the next train can pass and then  just fix it properly later  passing a monorail switch is also  excruciatingly slow whereas in europe  there are conventional rail switches  certified for 200 kilometers per hour  and that is not the speed for going  straight through it that's the speed at  which the actual switching between the  tracks happens  and so problem number three of monorails  is that they have to be completely  elevated  and so if you look at these schematics  comparing conventional rail to bottom  supported monorail to suspended monorail  we can see that monorail tracks  regardless of where they are require  a significant amount of track  infrastructure namely that they have to  be elevated even on the surface  and underground and this poses a rather  serious problem  you see surface level rail  infrastructure is the cheapest of the  three  and so when we build rail lines we try  to build as much as possible  on the surface but with monorail these  cost savings are impossible  because as you can see both on the  surface and underground you have to  build infrastructure  as if you are building elevated rail  with some corresponding construction  costs of course  so this means that over long distances  where you would lead the rails on the  ground  monorail is just not viable so if you  wanted to build a monorail line you  would need to find a section where  only elevated rails are possible which  in most cities is extremely rare  after all the vast majority of public  transit rail lines go either partially  underground or on the ground  but even if there was a section in your  city where only elevated lines were  possible  you still have to deal with problem  number four maintenance  now monorail maintenance itself is not  particularly costly  however as we discussed there are very  few places where it makes actual sense  to build a monorail  so if you have a transport company that  has say 10 light rail lines and they  want to build a new line between the  city center and the airport  and a fully elevated line would make  actual sense on that section  it still makes very little sense to  build a monorail line  because if you already have a  pre-existing light rail system you  should probably build a line where you  can run rolling stock you already have  otherwise you would need to invest in an  entire new maintenance depot in specific  repair equipment  retraining personnel to maintain the  monorail trains all this extra effort  and money  just for one single line and this is of  course something that no sensible  company would do  moving on the fifth biggest problem with  monorails seems to be capacity  now it is entirely possible to build a  monorail system capable of transporting  a lot of people  however the previously mentioned  problems relegate the monorail to very  specific locations  and because of this the monorail can not  really operate at high capacities  as mass transit should and so let's take  the sydney monorail as an example  it operated between 1988 and 2013  carrying 70 million passengers during  this time  the system had six trains running  between 8 stations  on a 3.6 kilometer long line now 70  million passengers over the course of 26  years sounds like a lot  and this also happens to be the number  of passengers that the budapest m3  metroline carries  in five months and it can do that by  having  six times more trains two times more  stations and a line which is about five  times longer and this produces a 70  times difference between ridership  numbers and all of this in a city which  is less than  third of sydney's size now the m3 line  has a ridership  similar to the line 3 of the chongqing  rail transit which is supposed to be the  busiest monorail line in the world  and the chongqing line 3 monorail can  achieve these numbers  by being four times longer and having  more than twice as many stations  now of course this is not meant to be a  direct comparison there are other  factors in place such as how the city is  laid out  where the line is led and so on but i  wanted to show you how  disproportionately low the monorail's  capacity is compared to conventional  rail  as in you have to build out a ton of  infrastructure even in a chinese  metropolis to surpass the passenger  account of a metro line in a 1.7 million  european city  so to recap the five main problems of  monorails are cross compatibility  switches the need for the track to be  completely elevated  the need for costly parallel maintenance  infrastructure  and low capacity compared to heavy rail  or even light rail  so why do some cities build monorails  well  mostly because they are considered to be  sleek and futuristic  even though you could achieve similar  passenger numbers on a proposed line by  just running two dozen of those little  rubber wheeled sightseeing trains  actually if you had a line where you ran  a dozen of those things and each carried  at least 50 people per hour for 12 hours  per day  you would produce the same annual  ridership numbers as the sydney monorail  monorails in general seem to be a  version of this stack bro  future transit drift which produces  overpriced  overhyped and yet very low capacity  transit which are at the end of the day  for the few and not for the many go  figure  and that's the reason why monorails are  a bad idea  alright so this was my hot take on  monorails  don't forget to like and subscribe and  if you feel like this content is worth  your money then there is a patreon link  down in the description  your patronage is very much appreciated  in the meantime  i'll be seeing you when i'll see you
 

